WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.330
Mark Hamill made it abundantly clear from
the start that he didn't agree with the direction

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:09.080
writer-director Rian Johnson wanted to take
Luke Skywalker in Star Wars: The Last Jedi.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:15.020
"When I read VII I told Rian I fundamentally
disagree with virtually everything you've

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.640
decided about my character."

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.140
And now that the movie is out, we can see
what he means.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.800
In an interview posted by YouTube user JarJar
Abrams, Hamill opened up about his problems

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:27.909
with Johnson's take on Skywalker and the character's
defeated mentality at the beginning of the

00:00:27.909 --> 00:00:28.409
film.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.920
"I said to Rian, 'Jedis don't give up.'

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.060
I mean, even if [Luke] had a problem, he would
maybe take a year to try and regroup.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.420
But if he made a mistake, he would try and
right that wrong.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.800
So, right there, we had a fundamental difference.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.360
But it's not my story anymore.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.060
It's somebody else's story, and Rian needed
me to be a certain way to make the ending

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:44.560
effective."

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.000
Hamill said Skywalker is so different in The
Last Jedi that he essentially considered Luke

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.020
to be a completely different character than
the one George Lucas created.

00:00:52.249 --> 00:00:56.320
When asked about one line that Luke says,
presumably that "the Jedi must end," Hamill

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:56.820
said,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.320
"That's the crux of my problem.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:00:59.500
Luke would never say that.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:00.240
I'm sorry.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.940
Well, in this version, see I'm talking about
the George Lucas Star Wars.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:08.080
This is the next generation of Star Wars,
so I almost had to think of Luke as another

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:08.580
character.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.040
Maybe he's Jake Skywalker.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.840
He's not my Luke Skywalker.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.280
But I had to do what Rian wanted me to do
because it serves the story well.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.020
But, listen, I still haven't accepted it completely."

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.960
Of course this interview is from before the
release of The Last Jedi, and Hamill ended

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.880
it on a pragmatic and positive note.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.020
"It's only a movie.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.060
I hope people like it.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.420
I hope they don't get upset.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.060
I came to believe that Rian is the exact man
that they needed for this job."

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.780
Asking fans not to get upset about The Last
Jedi?

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.160
Now that would be the greatest Jedi mind trick
Luke ever pulled.

